    "sectionId": "auto-repair:section:horn-and-wiper-system-repair",
    "title": "Diagnosing Auxiliary Control Systems",
    "body": "The horn system typically operates through a relay controlled by a steering wheel switch, which completes a ground circuit to energize the relay coil. When energized, the relay connects battery voltage to the horn assembly. Windshield wiper systems utilize a multi-speed motor often controlled by a body control module or a multi-function switch, incorporating a park switch to ensure blades return to the bottom position. Customers may report that the horn fails to sound, sounds continuously, or that wipers fail to park, operate at certain speeds, or move entirely. Diagnostic procedures begin with checking system fuses using a test light to verify power availability. Next, use a digital multimeter to measure voltage at the motor or horn connector while activating the switch. A missing ground at the relay or motor is a common finding. Repairs involve replacing failed relays, burnt motor brushes, or corroded electrical connectors. Verification requires testing operation across all switch positions and ensuring the wiper blades stop correctly at the base of the windshield. Always disconnect the battery negative terminal when working on internal steering column components to prevent accidental airbag deployment or electrical shorts.",
    "definition": "Auxiliary control systems refer to non-critical comfort or safety-related circuits like wipers and horns that share common electrical control principles.",
    "whyItMatters": "Properly functioning wipers and horns are essential for driver visibility and safety, requiring precise electrical troubleshooting skills.",
    "summary": "This lesson establishes methods for diagnosing auxiliary control systems by isolating power, ground, and signal inputs to identify the specific failed component.",
